If you keep notes, meeting write-ups and drafts in a workspace, you now have three different ways to add AI to them. You can pay for Notion's built-in AI, pay for a general assistant such as ChatGPT or Claude and paste work into it, or do both. The prices look similar on the surface, but they buy different things. This guide compares the published plans from Notion, OpenAI and Anthropic as of 11 November 2026, using list prices in US dollars from each vendor's own pages. A disclosure: this post was drafted by Claude, an AI assistant made by Anthropic, which is one of the vendors covered here, and it was checked by a separate AI reviewer against the vendor pages. Stackbrook did not test these tools, and has no affiliate relationship with any of them at the time of writing. The header image is AI-generated.

## The prices at a glance

| Product | Free | Entry paid plan | Higher plans |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Notion | Yes, AI trial only | Plus, $10 per member per month | Business, $20 per member per month billed annually, with AI included |
| ChatGPT | Yes, limited | Plus, $20 a month (launch announcement) | Pro, several usage tiers, $200 at launch |
| Claude | Yes, limited | Pro, $20 a month | Max, $100 a month, and Ultra, $200 a month |

Notion's prices are per member of a workspace, while ChatGPT and Claude are priced per person. Prices vary by region and billing period. Notion's pricing page has a monthly and yearly toggle and says you can save with yearly billing, and its startup page describes the Business price as $20 per member paid annually, so the $10 and $20 figures here should be read as annual-billing prices. Monthly billing costs more, so check the live toggle at checkout.

## Notion AI

Notion's AI is no longer sold as a separate add-on that you attach to a plan. On its pricing page Notion lists Plus at $10 per member a month and Business at $20, and describes AI usage on the Free and Plus plans as limited and intended for trying Notion AI. Its help page adds that upgrading from Free to Plus does not renew the AI usage you had, so a Plus workspace that wants ongoing AI has to move up a plan.

Business and Enterprise plans give each member two monthly credit allowances. Notion says each member gets 1,500 Standard credits, used where Notion picks the model, for example Notion Agent on Auto, and 500 Select credits, used where you pick the model or run your own Custom Agent. The help page says these do not roll over. When they run out, features that use them pause until the next billing cycle, unless a workspace owner has turned on extra Notion credits. Notion's pricing page lists Custom Agents as free to try and then $10 per 1,000 monthly Notion credits. Notion also says extra credits cannot be bought with an app-store subscription. To buy them, an owner must cancel the existing plan, wait for it to expire, and move billing to notion.com on a Business or Enterprise plan that includes AI, so read the credit rules before you plan on heavy usage.

One more detail from Notion's own blog: you can connect a ChatGPT Plus, Pro or Go account to your personal Notion Agent, which means a ChatGPT subscription can power some of the AI inside Notion. Notion says this works in the personal agent to start, not in Custom Agents.

## ChatGPT

OpenAI lists Free, Go, Plus and Pro plans for individuals. When it launched Go, OpenAI listed Go at $8 a month in the US, Plus at $20 and Pro at $200, and noted that Go pricing is localized in some markets. OpenAI's pricing page now describes Pro as having a choice of usage tiers, and a $100 Pro tier has been reported alongside the $200 one, so check the live pricing page for the tier and price you want. The dollar amounts for Go, Plus and Pro come from the launch announcement, not from the current pricing page.

For notes and documents, the useful parts are Projects, memory and file limits. OpenAI's help article says Projects are included with every qualifying plan, with 5 files per project on Free, 25 on Go and Plus, and 40 on Pro, Business and Enterprise. It also says a project can use default memory or project-only memory. OpenAI's pricing page describes memory and context as limited on Free, longer on Go, expanded on Plus and maximum on Pro. OpenAI does not give numbers for those tiers.

## Claude

Anthropic lists Pro at $20 a month, or $17 a month when billed annually with $200 up front, Max at $100 a month and Ultra at $200 a month. Its pricing page lists memory across conversations on the Free plan, and Projects among the Free and Pro plan features. Anthropic's help pages say Pro and Max have a five-hour limit plus a weekly limit, and that Ultra has no five-hour limit for verified accounts, so there is no fixed number of messages to plan around. Anthropic's help page says the Pro plan offers more usage than Free but the number of messages varies with message length, attached files and the model you use.

## What you are actually choosing between

- **Notion AI** works inside your pages and databases. It can summarize meetings, fill database columns and answer questions using your workspace, but it is metered in credits and the full set sits on Business.
- **ChatGPT and Claude** are general assistants. They may suit drafting, rewriting or analyzing text from many sources. Their individual plans are priced per person, so ten people each paying $20 is also about $200 a month, and both vendors price team plans separately.
- **Both** is possible. Notion can hold the notes while a separate assistant does the drafting, and Notion says you can connect a ChatGPT subscription to its personal agent.

## Which to pick

- If you are a solo user who writes mostly outside a workspace, a $20 ChatGPT Plus or Claude Pro plan may be enough for drafting and summarizing, and the free tiers let you try both.
- If you already live in Notion with a team, compare the cost of the Business plan for every member against what the AI saves. Ten members on Business is $200 a month, or $2,400 a year, at the annual-billing price, before any extra credits, and more on monthly billing.
- If you only need an occasional summary inside Notion, the Free or Plus trial may be enough to test it, but it is not a continuing allowance.
- If you already pay for ChatGPT, check whether connecting it to Notion gives you the Notion features you wanted before you buy Business.

Before paying, try the free tier of each tool on your own notes, and check what usage information each one shows after a week. Plans and credit rules change, so check the live pricing pages first.
